course: dictionary
lesson: 12091
title: When separate things join together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe things that start apart and then become one. Coalesce is one of them. It means to come together and join to form one larger thing or group. The ending -esce is a sign of formal English, so you will mostly meet this word in science books, history and the news.
explain: «Tiny droplets in the cloud slowly coalesced until they were heavy enough to fall as rain.» The droplets started out separate, then joined into bigger drops. That is exactly what coalesce means.
explain: Check with the swap test. Put «join together» in place of coalesce: «Tiny droplets in the cloud slowly joined together until they were heavy enough to fall as rain.» The sentence still makes sense, so you have found the meaning. Formal writing uses coalesce; everyday English says join together or merge.
fact: Coalesce is a verb. Its other forms are coalesces, coalesced and coalescing.
fact: Coalesce means to come together and join to form one larger thing or group.
fact: In science, drops, particles or bubbles can coalesce, which means they merge into one.
fact: «Raindrops coalesce in clouds» shows tiny drops joining until they become heavy enough to fall.
fact: Everyday words for coalesce are join together and merge.
exercise: «Tiny droplets in the clouds coalesce until they are heavy enough to fall as rain.» What does coalesce mean here? | Freeze into hard ice | Float away from the clouds | Split apart into smaller drops | *Join together to form bigger drops
exercise: «Over millions of years, dust and gas coalesced to make stars.» Which everyday words could replace coalesced? | Ran away | Shrank | Got lost | *Joined together
exercise: «The separate groups coalesced into one movement for change.» What happened to the groups? | They stopped talking to each other | They moved to different towns | They split into many small groups | *They came together and became one movement
test: «Water droplets coalesced on the window, and one large drop ran down the glass.» What does coalesced mean? | Broke into tiny pieces | Dried up | Turned into steam | *Came together to form one larger drop
test: «Over time, small villages coalesced into one large town.» Which sentence means the same? | The town broke into small villages | The villages moved away from the town | The villages became smaller | *The villages joined together to form one town
exam: Ruby says coalesce is a posh way to say join together. Is she right? | No, it only describes sounds | No, it means to break apart | No, it means to freeze solid | *Yes, it means much the same as join together, but it is more formal
